\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The increment satisfies the triangle inequality}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{The \vocabk{increment metric}{fdip-00CY} is symmetric and
vanishes when its two arguments agree. It obeys the triangle
inequality. If #{\mathbb E\lvert f_x-f_y\rvert^2>0} whenever
#{x\ne y}, then it is a metric.}

\proof{
\p{Symmetry and the diagonal are immediate from the formula.
For the triangle inequality, #{f_x-f_z=(f_x-f_y)+(f_y-f_z)}.
The square root of an expectation of a square is the #{L^2}
norm of the random variable, so the usual triangle inequality
in #{L^2} gives #{d(x,z)\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)}. The last hypothesis
makes #{d(x,y)=0} only when #{x=y}.}
}
